WEBVTT

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:04.055
Digital signatures are part of the quantum threat discussion because they protect

00:00:04.055 --> 00:00:04.680
identity,

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.960
integrity, authenticity, and trust.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.610
A signature can prove that a software update came from the expected publisher,

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:14.360
that firmware was approved, that a certificate was issued by a trusted authority,

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.710
or that a document was not changed after signing.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:19.540
These uses are deeply embedded in security operations.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:21.440
Certificate chains are especially important.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:25.200
A certificate chain links identities to public keys through trusted issuers.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.480
If the signature algorithms used in those chains become unsuitable,

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.745
organizations need a migration path for certificate authorities, clients, servers,

00:00:32.745 --> 00:00:35.478
devices, verification libraries, and policy rules.

00:00:35.478 --> 00:00:37.460
That migration is not only technical.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:41.645
It may involve contracts, audits, governance, procurement, and operational change windows.

00:00:41.645 --> 00:00:45.520
Code signing and firmware signing can be harder than changing an encryption setting.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:47.460
Devices may validate signatures offline.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:51.010
Boot chains may depend on algorithms burned into firmware or hardware.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:54.920
Update systems may require compatibility across multiple product generations.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:59.230
Some devices stay deployed for years in medical, industrial, automotive, defense,

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:02.930
or infrastructure environments where patching is slow and replacement is expensive.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:06.309
The practical question is where trust depends on long-lived signatures.

00:01:06.309 --> 00:01:10.120
Signed logs, software packages, firmware images, documents, certificates,

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:14.645
and device identities may all have different validity periods and operational constraints.

00:01:14.645 --> 00:01:17.810
Signature migration requires understanding verification behavior,

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:19.100
not just signing behavior.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:23.520
A new signing algorithm helps only if every relying system can verify it correctly.
